{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1481536-the-children-of-non-animal-pangeans","id":1481536,"title":"The Children of Non-Animal Pangeans","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Werewolf: The Forsaken"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-05-20T08:46:37-07:00","id":1481536,"title":"The Children of Non-Animal Pangeans","content":"I am wondering what kind of children the pangeans without a direct easy physical analogue to use would have(so discounting plants and stone here as they could just have golem shifters or dryads or w/e). Like what would the child of the pangean god of the north wind, or the pangean of fire, and similar. Of course, I am talking about counterparts to the Uratha here, but I was interested in hearing what you guys think could come of these more conceptual pangeans.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8100-caedus","name":"Caedus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"300"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-23T14:45:49-07:00","id":1481781,"title":"","content":"One of the most straight-forward (and in my opinion appropriate) ways to handle it is to use a symbolic creature, especially from myth and folklore, and build from there. Remember, Urfarah is the god of the Hunt (a fairly abstract concept) - symbolized as Wolf - so it's very appropriate to use mythic beasts to embody them. Another approach is to lean into the intangible qualities - it's perfectly alright for their hybrid children to be intangible, invisible, spirit-like things (kind of using the Strix as a model for a material-but-intangible being). A third approach would be follow Chris Allen's semi-official write up of <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/855280-pangaea-unleashed?p=859687#post859687\">Harvest</a> to describe physical experiences ascribed to the concept.<br /> <br /> Here\u2019s a few concepts I've been brainstorming:<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Song Birds, Children of Joy</b><div style=\"margin-left:40px\">Azure and Elusive Joy has long struggled in the hunter\u2019s paradise of Pangaea, flitting ceaselessly across the worlds, chased ever onward by Fear and Hunger, leaving surprising vistas of awe-inspiring artistry behind it. Paradoxically, it discovered that it\u2019s most lasting gift to the world was the fleeting notes of Song.<br /> <br /> It is said that Joy sought many partners through the Pangaean Pantheon - Butterfly, Rose - but that Bird was its deepest love. The cruel huntress was a surprising mate, but songbirds became the most iconic and pervasive of Joy\u2019s children.<br /> <br /> The true inheritors of Joy are marked by deep blue plumage, very nearly sparkling, and are most often seen carrying a golden bead - if they are seen at all. Like their progenitor, the Blue Birds of Happiness are vanishingly rare and elusive, but impart revelatory awe to those they visit. Where their song can be heard, colors are more vibrant, objects become lighter, and even monsters are lulled into (very brief) contentment; those who collect their feathers are inspired in art and play; but to catch a glimpse of the bird itself is to have a profound and lasting source of joy in your life revealed - a place to call home, a calling of passion, a beloved person. One of the most common experiences of those who see the Blue Birds is to fall in love soon after, so suddenly and so deeply, it\u2019s as if struck with a spear.<br /> <br /> Little known to even the Wise, the golden bead they carry are crystallized essence - the captured feeling of \u201csunshine in the heart\u201d. The songbirds seek out moments of spontaneous joy, and capture the spirits birthed by it, their bead glowing brighter with each one. When it reaches its most brilliant, it is embedded into the egg of a songbird, and a new hybrid is soon born, leading some to believe that the bauble itself is the shard of the Pangaean; the birds \u201cmerely\u201d it\u2019s Claimed devotees.</div><b>Salamanders, Children of Fire</b><div style=\"margin-left:40px\">The God of Flame, young and impetuous, is often times a gambler. It\u2019s own life cycle flickers continually between spark, inferno, and slumber; its children in Flesh and Shadow embody the phrase \u201clive fast, die young.\u201d Dissatisfied with living in spurts, it spawned a hybrid species, not to keep its fires burning, but to coax the world into tinder.<br /> <br /> How it was able to birth a beast remains a great mystery, but it\u2019s children are often spawned from the campfires of humans, crawling unharmed from the flame to begin their slow work. These salamanders desiccate the world around them, pulling moisture from wood and cloth, such that any stray spark will catch flame.<br /> <br /> Perhaps these monsters were all hunted down during the age of Pangaea, perhaps they couldn\u2019t endure the Sundering, but they seemed to have vanished rather quickly. Fire instead found its greatest payoffs from its spirit child, Hearth, who partnered with humanity to become a patron of mysticism, technology, and protection - and who now has flames that never burn out. But, for a time, the salamander hybrids were heralds of the great infernos, a subtle and surprisingly self-aware scheme in Fire\u2019s ancient reign.</div><b>Whispers, Children of the Wind</b><div style=\"margin-left:40px\">Tempest, goddess of Storms, rages unpredictably between the worlds. Few suspect him of subtlety, but for every terrifying second of cyclones or thunderstorms where xe manifests, a dozen of her children linger for generations: for every act to create a raging wind (including merely manifesting) also Creates both physical and spiritual children. But who would expect him of invoking Desire?<br /> <br /> Perhaps frustrated that the Astral was a realm thon struggled to sustain herself in, Wind\u2019s hybrid children are goetic beings foremost, where they have become the messengers of Longing.<br /> <br /> Whispers intrude frequently into the physical world to literally carry something on the wind. In the time of Pangaea, they were shockingly pervasive and active monsters, bearing fragrances and echoes; warmth from the equatorial deserts, frost from the northern wastes - messages from distant lands. Where there wasn\u2019t such essence to sow, they became \u2018pranksters\u2019, surprising creatures with unexpected scents and sounds that sparked curiosity and fear.<br /> <br /> They became most entrenched, however, in bearing messages to distant places: claims of territory roared across the forest; prayers of hope offered to travelers. Wise of the ancient world seized upon this role, and bound the Whispers to rites of communication - carrying messages across great distances. As the Gauntlet sundered the worlds, this gave them refuge in the Astral - and means of manifestation in the Flesh. In subtle ways, they power the magics that convey knowledge across distances, and in so doing become bridges between Astral Realms: the causeways that link oneiros to shared knowledge.</div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">Second Chance for<br /> <img class=\"bbcode-attachment js-need-data-att-update\" src=\"filedata/fetch?filedataid=709\" data-tempid=\"temp_709_1572548741915_354\" /><br /> A Beautiful Madness</div>","edited":{"userid":425,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/425-seraph-kitty","user":"Seraph Kitty","date":". 05-23-2022, 05:54 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/425-seraph-kitty","name":"Seraph Kitty","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar425_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"394"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}